<figure class="buffer-diagram" aria-label="Two ways file data reaches the volume">
  <div class="buffer-route">
    <div class="route-label"><strong>Disk buffer</strong><span>Default</span></div>
    <div class="data-path">
      <div class="endpoint"><code>write</code><small>Your application</small></div>
      <div class="connector"><span>direct block I/O</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></div>
      <div class="endpoint volume"><strong>OPFS volume</strong><small>File data blocks</small></div>
    </div>
    <p>No full RAM copy of file contents. Synchronization flushes pending data and metadata.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="buffer-route">
    <div class="route-label"><strong>Memory buffer</strong><span>Optional</span></div>
    <div class="data-path memory-path">
      <div class="endpoint"><code>write</code><small>Your application</small></div>
      <div class="connector"><span>cache</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></div>
      <div class="endpoint"><strong>RAM</strong><small>File contents</small></div>
      <div class="connector"><span>copy at sync</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></div>
      <div class="endpoint volume"><strong>OPFS volume</strong><small>File data blocks</small></div>
    </div>
    <p class="log-branch"><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span> Each write also appends to an <strong>OPFS recovery log</strong>. On recovery, the VFS replays recoverable records into RAM.</p>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Both modes use persistent OPFS storage. These are file-data paths, not flush guarantees or a complete view of metadata and bookkeeping.</figcaption>
</figure>

